<worksheet xml:id="sec-quiz-sampling-phat-att-1">
  <title>Quiz: Sampling Distribution of <m>\widehat{p}</m> Attempt 1</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>Name: <fillin characters="45"/></p>

    <assemblage>
      <p><em>Sampling distribution formulas</em></p>
      <p>
        <m>\widehat{p}=\frac{x}{n}</m>
        <m>\qquad</m>
        <m>\mu_{\widehat{p}}=p</m>
        <m>\qquad</m>
        <m>\sigma_{\widehat{p}}=\sqrt{\frac{p(1-p)}{n}}</m>
        <m>\qquad</m>
        <m>z=\frac{\widehat{p}-\mu_{\widehat{p}}}{\sigma_{\widehat{p}}}
        =\frac{\widehat{p}-p}{\sqrt{\frac{p(1-p)}{n}}}</m>
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      Show your setup or the R command used. Round probabilities to four decimal
      places. Round z-scores, standard deviations, and cutoff values to two
      decimal places.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-quiz-sampling-phat-att-1-model" workspace="1.25in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that 64% of the 8,000 adult frogs in a wetland carry a
        particular harmless skin bacterium. A simple random sample of 200 frogs
        is selected without replacement, and 138 of the sampled frogs carry
        the bacterium. Let <m>\widehat{p}</m> be the sample proportion of frogs
        that carry the bacterium.
      </p>
      <ol marker="a)">
        <li><p>Calculate the observed sample proportion <m>\widehat{p}</m>.</p></li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Check the conditions for using the usual normal model for the
            sampling distribution of <m>\widehat{p}</m>. Include numerical
            checks where appropriate.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li><p>Find the mean and standard deviation of the sampling distribution of <m>\widehat{p}</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Find the <m>z</m>-score of the observed sample proportion and interpret it in context.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-quiz-sampling-phat-att-1-probabilities" workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that 30% of the 24,000 seedlings in a nursery have a
        particular leaf disease. A simple random sample of 240 seedlings is
        selected without replacement. Let <m>\widehat{p}</m> be the proportion
        of sampled seedlings that have the disease. The conditions for the
        usual normal model are satisfied.
      </p>
      <ol marker="a)">
        <li><p>Find the probability that more than 35% of the sampled seedlings have the disease.</p></li>
        <li><p>Would a sample proportion above 0.35 be unusual? Use a probability below 0.05 as the criterion and explain.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-quiz-sampling-phat-att-1-cutoff" workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that 25% of the 40,000 wetlands in a region contain a
        particular invasive plant. A simple random sample of 400 wetlands is
        selected without replacement. Let <m>\widehat{p}</m> be the proportion
        of sampled wetlands that contain the plant.
      </p>
      <p>
        Find the cutoff <m>c</m> for the highest 10% of sample proportions.
        Write a probability statement that describes this cutoff, and give
        the cutoff as a proportion between 0 and 1.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
